**Sec. 50101.** This section repeals specific Inflation Reduction Act provisions regarding onshore oil and gas royalty rates and noncompetitive leasing, restoring prior laws. It mandates the Secretary of the Interior to immediately resume quarterly onshore oil and gas lease sales in nine specified states, requiring at least 50% of available nominated parcels to be offered in each sale. The Mineral Leasing Act is amended to require parcels to be made available for leasing within 18 months of an expression of interest and to allow commingling of production from multiple sources if measurement uncertainty is within plus or minus 2 percent.

**Sec. 50102.** The Secretary of the Interior is required to conduct a minimum of 30 region-wide oil and gas lease sales in the Gulf of America and 6 offshore lease sales in the Cook Inlet Planning Area, Alaska, with specific timing schedules extending through 2040. For Gulf sales, the royalty rate is capped between 12.5% and 16 2/3%, and leases in water depths of 800 meters or deeper must have a 10-year primary term. Alaska offshore revenues are split 70% to the State of Alaska and 30% to the Treasury starting in fiscal year 2034, while the section also repeals Inflation Reduction Act offshore royalty provisions and adjusts revenue distribution limits for the Gulf of Mexico Energy Security Act.

**Sec. 50103.** This section repeals Section 50263 of Public Law 117-169, which had established royalties on extracted methane.

**Sec. 50104.** The Secretary of the Interior must conduct at least four area-wide lease sales in the Alaska Coastal Plain within ten years of enactment, offering no fewer than 400,000 acres per sale with terms matching the 2020 Record of Decision. Revenue sharing from these leases allocates 50% to the State of Alaska for fiscal years 2025 through 2033, increasing to 70% for Alaska starting in fiscal year 2034, with the remainder deposited into the U.S. Treasury.

**Sec. 50105.** The Secretary of the Interior is directed to restore and resume oil and gas lease sales in the National Petroleum Reserve-Alaska (NPR-A), conducting at least five sales over ten years with each sale offering no fewer than 4,000,000 acres. Lease terms and stipulations must align with the 2020 NPR-A Record of Decision. Starting in fiscal year 2034, 70% of receipts from NPR-A leases issued after enactment will be paid to the State of Alaska, with the remaining 30% going to the U.S. Treasury.

**Sec. 50201.** Within 90 days of enactment, the Secretary of the Interior must process all qualified coal lease applications by publishing environmental reviews, establishing fair market value, holding lease sales, and granting leases to the highest bidders. The Secretary may also grant additional approvals for previously issued coal leases to commence mining activities.

**Sec. 50202.** The coal royalty rate under the Mineral Leasing Act is reduced from 12.5% to 7% for the period beginning on the date of enactment and ending September 30, 2034. This reduced rate applies to all coal leases issued before, on, or after enactment that have not been terminated. Lessees who paid advance royalties before enactment are entitled to a credit for the difference between the amount paid and what would have been required under the new 7% rate.

**Sec. 50203.** The Secretary of the Interior must make available for lease at least 4,000,000 additional acres of known recoverable coal resources on Federal land in the 48 contiguous States and Alaska within 90 days of enactment. These lands are excluded if they are within National Monuments, Wilderness Areas, Wild and Scenic Rivers, National Trails, Conservation Areas, Wildlife Refuges, Fish Hatcheries, or National Parks.

**Sec. 50204.** Federal coal reserves located in Federal land with previously approved mining plans and adjacent to State or private land are authorized to be mined to ensure economic access to adjacent reserves. The Secretary of the Interior must take necessary steps to authorize this mining within 90 days of enactment without substantial modification to the existing plans. This authorization does not prevent reviews under the National Environmental Policy Act.

**Sec. 50301.** The Secretary of Agriculture must sell timber on National Forest System land in quantities at least 250,000,000 board-feet greater than the previous year for fiscal years 2026 through 2034, while the Secretary of the Interior must sell timber on BLM public lands in quantities at least 20,000,000 board-feet greater than the previous year for the same period. Additionally, the Forest Service must enter into at least 40 long-term timber sale contracts and the BLM at least 5 long-term vegetative material disposal contracts, each with a minimum term of 20 years, with all receipts deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 50302.** Holders of rights-of-way for renewable energy projects on Federal land must pay annual acreage rent calculated using a formula based on local pastureland rental rates, an encumbrance factor (100% for solar, at least 10% for wind), and a 3% annual adjustment factor. Once energy generation begins, holders must pay an annual capacity fee equal to the greater of the acreage rent or 3.9% of gross electricity sales proceeds. Wind energy projects may request a 10% reduction in the capacity fee if at least 25% of the land is used for other authorized activities, and rights-of-way may be terminated for non-payment after 90 days.

**Sec. 50303.** Beginning January 1, 2026, revenues from renewable energy projects on covered Federal land, including bonus bids, rentals, and fees, will be deposited into the general fund of the Treasury. Of these funds, 25% will be allocated to the State where the revenue is derived, and 25% will be allocated to counties within that State based on the percentage of county land from which the revenue is derived. These payments are in addition to existing payments in lieu of taxes and must be made available in the fiscal year following collection.

**Sec. 50304.** This section rescinds the unobligated balances of funds made available under Sections 50221, 50222, and 50223 of the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022, which pertained to National Park Service and Bureau of Land Management programs.

**Sec. 50305.** The Secretary of the Interior, acting through the National Park Service, is appropriated $150,000,000 for fiscal year 2025 to fund events, celebrations, and activities commemorating the 250th anniversary of the founding of the United States. These funds remain available through fiscal year 2028.

**Sec. 50401.** The Department of Energy is appropriated $218,000,000 for maintenance and repairs of Strategic Petroleum Reserve facilities and $171,000,000 to acquire petroleum products for storage, with funds available until September 30, 2029. The section also repeals Section 20003 of Public Law 115-97, which had mandated a drawdown and sale of the Strategic Petroleum Reserve.

**Sec. 50402.** This section repeals Section 50142 of the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022 and rescinds the unobligated balances of funds made available under that section. It also rescinds unobligated balances from eight other specific sections of the Inflation Reduction Act, including Sections 50123, 50141, 50144, 50145, 50151, 50152, 50153, and 50161.

**Sec. 50403.** The Energy Policy Act of 2005 is amended to expand the scope of energy dominance financing to include activities that increase capacity or output and support grid reliability, while removing previous restrictions on loan guarantees. The Secretary is appropriated $1,000,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2028, to carry out these activities, with no more than 3% usable for administrative expenses. Additionally, the commitment authority deadline under the Inflation Reduction Act is extended from 2026 to 2028.

**Sec. 50404.** The Secretary of Energy is directed to mobilize National Laboratories to curate Department of Energy scientific data for use in artificial intelligence and machine learning models and to initiate seed efforts for self-improving AI models for science and engineering. The curated data may be used to develop next-generation microelectronics and new energy technologies, with AI models provided to the scientific community via the "American science cloud." The section appropriates $150,000,000, available through September 30, 2026, to carry out these activities.

**Sec. 50501.** The Secretary of the Interior, acting through the Commissioner of Reclamation, is appropriated $1,000,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2034, to restore or increase the capacity of existing Bureau of Reclamation conveyance and surface water storage facilities. Contracts entered into under this section are not treated as new or amended contracts for purposes of certain Reclamation Acts, and the funds are not subject to reimbursement, matching, or cost-sharing requirements. [end of text]


